sn5.6:1.6 | S: “I don’t approve of rebirth, sir.” | T: “I don’t approve of birth, friend.”
sn5.6:2.1 | S: “Why don’t you approve of rebirth? | T: “Why don’t you approve of birth?
sn5.6:2.2 | S: When you’re born, you get to enjoy sensual pleasures. | T: Once born, one enjoys sensual pleasures.
sn5.6:2.3 | S: Who put this idea in your head: | T: Who now has persuaded you of this:
sn5.6:2.4 | S: ‘Nun, don’t approve of rebirth’?” | T: ‘Bhikkhuni, don’t approve of birth’?”
sn5.6:3.1 | S: “Death comes to those who are born, | T: The bhikkhuni Cala: “For one who is born there is death;
sn5.6:3.2 | S: when you’re born you undergo sufferings— | T: Once born, one encounters sufferings—
sn5.6:3.3 | S: killing, caging, misery— | T: Bondage, murder, affliction—
sn5.6:3.4 | S: that’s why you shouldn’t approve of rebirth. | T: Hence one shouldn’t approve of birth.
sn5.6:4.1 | S: The Buddha taught me the Dhamma | T: “The Buddha has taught the Dhamma,
sn5.6:4.2 | S: for transcending rebirth, | T: The transcendence of birth;
sn5.6:4.3 | S: for giving up all suffering; | T: For the abandoning of all suffering
sn5.6:4.4 | S: he settled me in the truth. | T: He has settled me in the truth.
sn5.6:5.1 | S: There are beings in the realm of luminous form, | T: “As to those beings who fare amidst form,
sn5.6:5.2 | S: and others stuck in the formless. | T: And those who abide in the formless—
sn5.6:5.3 | S: Not understanding cessation, | T: Not having understood cessation,
sn5.6:5.4 | S: they return in future lives.” | T: They come again to renewed existence.”
sn5.6:6.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The nun Cālā knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, realizing, “The bhikkhuni Cala knows me,” sad and disappointed, disappeared right there.

