sn5.8:4.1 | S: But there is one born in the Sakyan clan, | T: “But there’s one born in the Sakyan clan,
sn5.8:4.2 | S: the unrivaled Buddha, | T: The Enlightened One, without an equal,
sn5.8:4.3 | S: vanquisher of all, dispeller of Māra, | T: Conqueror of all, Mara’s subduer,
sn5.8:4.4 | S: everywhere undefeated, | T: Who everywhere is undefeated,
sn5.8:5.1 | S: everywhere freed, and unattached, | T: Everywhere freed and unattached,
sn5.8:5.2 | S: the Clear-eyed One sees all. | T: The One with Vision who sees all.
sn5.8:5.3 | S: He has attained the end of all deeds, | T: “Attained to the end of all kamma,
sn5.8:5.4 | S: freed with the ending of attachments. | T: Liberated in the extinction of acquisitions,
sn5.8:5.5 | S: That Blessed One is my Teacher, | T: That Blessed One is my Teacher:
sn5.8:5.6 | S: and I approve his instruction.” | T: His is the teaching I approve.”
sn5.8:6.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The nun Sīsupacālā knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One, realizing, “The bhikkhuni Sisupacala knows me,” sad and disappointed, disappeared right there.

## sn56.10
sn56.10:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, don’t engage in all kinds of low talk, such as | T: “Bhikkhus, do not engage in the various kinds of pointless talk, that is,
sn56.10:1.2 | S: talk about kings, bandits, and chief ministers; talk about armies, threats, and wars; talk about food, drink, clothes, and beds; talk about garlands and fragrances; talk about family, vehicles, villages, towns, cities, and countries; talk about women and heroes; talk at the street market and the place for water pots; talk about the departed; motley talk; tales of land and sea; and talk about being reborn in this or that place. | T: talk about kings, thieves, and ministers of state; talk about armies, dangers, and wars; talk about food, drink, garments, and beds; talk about garlands and scents; talk about relations, vehicles, villages, towns, cities, and countries; talk about women and talk about heroes; street talk and talk by the well; talk about those departed in days gone by; rambling chitchat; speculation about the world and about the sea; talk about becoming this or that.
sn56.10:1.3 | S: Why is that? | T: For what reason?
sn56.10:1.4 | S: Because those discussions aren’t beneficial or relevant to the fundamentals of the spiritual life. They don’t lead to disillusionment, dispassion, cessation, peace, insight, awakening, and extinguishment. | T: Because, bhikkhus, this talk is unbeneficial, irrelevant to the fundamentals of the holy life, and does not lead to revulsion, to dispassion, to cessation, to peace, to direct knowledge, to enlightenment, to Nibbāna.
