## sn56.11
sn56.11:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana. | T: Thus have I heard. On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling at Baraṇasi in the Deer Park at Isipatana.
sn56.11:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the group of five mendicants: | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus of the group of five thus:
sn56.11:2.1 | S: “Mendicants, these two extremes should not be cultivated by one gone forth. | T: “Bhikkhus, these two extremes should not be followed by one who has gone forth into homelessness.
sn56.11:2.2 | S: What two? | T: What two?
sn56.11:2.3 | S: Indulgence in sensual pleasures, which is low, crude, ordinary, ignoble, and pointless. And indulgence in self-mortification, which is painful, ignoble, and pointless. | T: The pursuit of sensual happiness in sensual pleasures, which is low, vulgar, the way of worldlings, ignoble, unbeneficial; and the pursuit of self-mortification, which is painful, ignoble, unbeneficial.
sn56.11:2.4 | S: Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One understood the middle way of practice, which gives vision and knowledge, and leads to peace, direct knowledge, awakening, and extinguishment. | T: Without veering towards either of these extremes, the Tathagata has awakened to the middle way, which gives rise to vision, which gives rise to knowledge, which leads to peace, to direct knowledge, to enlightenment, to Nibbāna.
sn56.11:3.1 | S: And what is that middle way of practice? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is that middle way awakened to by the Tathagata, which gives rise to vision … which leads to Nibbāna?
sn56.11:3.2 | S: It is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: It is this Noble Eightfold Path; that is,
sn56.11:3.3 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn56.11:3.4 | S: This is that middle way of practice, which gives vision and knowledge, and leads to peace, direct knowledge, awakening, and extinguishment. | T: This, bhikkhus, is that middle way awakened to by the Tathagata, which gives rise to vision, which gives rise to knowledge, which leads to peace, to direct knowledge, to enlightenment, to Nibbāna.
sn56.11:4.1 | S: Now this is the noble truth of suffering. | T: “Now this, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of suffering:
sn56.11:4.2 | S: Rebirth is suffering; old age is suffering; illness is suffering; death is suffering; being coupled with the disliked is suffering; separation from the liked is suffering; not getting what you wish for is suffering. In brief, the five grasping aggregates are suffering. | T: birth is suffering, aging is suffering, illness is suffering, death is suffering; union with what is displeasing is suffering; separation from what is pleasing is suffering; not to get what one wants is suffering; in brief, the five aggregates subject to clinging are suffering.
sn56.11:4.3 | S: Now this is the noble truth of the origin of suffering. | T: “Now this, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of the origin of suffering:
sn56.11:4.4 | S: It’s the craving that leads to future lives, mixed up with relishing and greed, taking pleasure there wherever it alights. That is, | T: it is this craving which leads to renewed existence, accompanied by delight and lust, seeking delight here and there; that is,
sn56.11:4.5 | S: craving for sensual pleasures, craving for continued existence, and craving for nonexistence. | T: craving for sensual pleasures, craving for existence, craving for extermination.
sn56.11:4.6 | S: Now this is the noble truth of the cessation of suffering. | T: “Now this, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of the cessation of suffering:
sn56.11:4.7 | S: It’s the fading away and cessation of that very same craving no residue left behind; giving it away, letting it go, releasing it, and not clinging to it. | T: it is the remainderless fading away and cessation of that same craving, the giving up and relinquishing of it, freedom from it, nonreliance on it.
sn56.11:4.8 | S: Now this is the noble truth of the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: “Now this, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering:
sn56.11:4.9 | S: It is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: it is this Noble Eightfold Path; that is,
sn56.11:4.10 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view … right concentration.
sn56.11:5.1 | S: ‘This is the noble truth of suffering.’ Such was the vision, knowledge, wisdom, realization, and light that arose in me regarding teachings not learned before from another. | T: “‘This is the noble truth of suffering’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:5.2 | S: ‘This noble truth of suffering should be completely understood.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This noble truth of suffering is to be fully understood’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:5.3 | S: ‘This noble truth of suffering has been completely understood.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This noble truth of suffering has been fully understood’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:6.1 | S: ‘This is the noble truth of the origin of suffering.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This is the noble truth of the origin of suffering’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:6.2 | S: ‘This noble truth of the origin of suffering should be given up.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This noble truth of the origin of suffering is to be abandoned’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:6.3 | S: ‘This noble truth of the origin of suffering has been given up.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This noble truth of the origin of suffering has been abandoned’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:7.1 | S: ‘This is the noble truth of the cessation of suffering.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This is the noble truth of the cessation of suffering’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:7.2 | S: ‘This noble truth of the cessation of suffering should be realized.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This noble truth of the cessation of suffering is to be realized’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:7.3 | S: ‘This noble truth of the cessation of suffering has been realized.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This noble truth of the cessation of suffering has been realized’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:8.1 | S: ‘This is the noble truth of the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This is the noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:8.2 | S: ‘This noble truth of the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering should be developed.’ Such was the vision that arose in me … | T: “‘This noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering is to be developed’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:8.3 | S: ‘This noble truth of the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering has been developed.’ Such was the vision, knowledge, wisdom, realization, and light that arose in me regarding teachings not learned before from another. | T: “‘This noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering has been developed’: thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.
sn56.11:9.1 | S: As long as my true knowledge and vision about these four noble truths was not fully purified in these three rounds and twelve aspects, I didn’t announce my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: “So long, bhikkhus, as my knowledge and vision of these Four Noble Truths as they really are in their three phases and twelve aspects was not thoroughly purified in this way, I did not claim to have awakened to the unsurpassed perfect enlightenment in this world with its devas, Mara, and Brahma, in this generation with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans.
sn56.11:10.1 | S: But when my true knowledge and vision about these four noble truths was fully purified in these three rounds and twelve aspects, I announced my supreme perfect awakening in this world with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans. | T: But when my knowledge and vision of these Four Noble Truths as they really are in their three phases and twelve aspects was thoroughly purified in this way, then I claimed to have awakened to the unsurpassed perfect enlightenment in this world with its devas, Mara, and Brahma, in this generation with its ascetics and brahmins, its devas and humans.
sn56.11:10.2 | S: Knowledge and vision arose in me: | T: The knowledge and vision arose in me:
sn56.11:10.3 | S: ‘My freedom is unshakable; this is my last rebirth; now there’ll be no more future lives.’” | T: ‘Unshakable is the liberation of my mind. This is my last birth. Now there is no more renewed existence.’”
sn56.11:10.4 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn56.11:10.5 | S: Satisfied, the group of five mendicants approved what the Buddha said. | T: Elated, the bhikkhus of the group of five delighted in the Blessed One’s statement.
sn56.11:11.1 | S: And while this discourse was being spoken, the stainless, immaculate vision of the Dhamma arose in Venerable Koṇḍañña: | T: And while this discourse was being spoken, there arose in the Venerable Kondañña the dust-free, stainless vision of the Dhamma:
sn56.11:11.2 | S: “Everything that is liable to arise is liable to cease.” | T: “Whatever is subject to origination is all subject to cessation.”
sn56.11:12.1 | S: And when the Buddha rolled forth the Wheel of Dhamma, the earth gods raised the cry: | T: And when the Wheel of the Dhamma had been set in motion by the Blessed One, the earth-dwelling devas raised a cry:
sn56.11:12.2 | S: “Near Varanasi, in the deer park at Isipatana, the Buddha has rolled forth the supreme Wheel of Dhamma. And that wheel cannot be rolled back by any ascetic or brahmin or god or Māra or divinity or by anyone in the world.” | T: “At Baraṇasi, in the Deer Park at Isipatana, this unsurpassed Wheel of the Dhamma has been set in motion by the Blessed One, which cannot be stopped by any ascetic or brahmin or deva or Mara or Brahma or by anyone in the world.”
sn56.11:12.3 | S: Hearing the cry of the earth gods, the gods of the four great kings … | T: Having heard the cry of the earth-dwelling devas, the devas of the realm of the Four Great Kings raised a cry:
sn56.11:12.4 | P: “etaṁ bhagavatā bārāṇasiyaṁ isipatane migadāye anuttaraṁ dhammacakkaṁ pavattitaṁ, appaṭivattiyaṁ samaṇena vā brāhmaṇena vā devena vā mārena vā brahmunā vā kenaci vā lokasmin”ti. | T: “At Baraṇasi … this unsurpassed Wheel of the Dhamma has been set in motion by the Blessed One, which cannot be stopped … by anyone in the world.”
sn56.11:12.5 | S: the gods of the thirty-three … | T: Having heard the cry of the devas of the realm of the Four Great Kings, the Tavatiṁsa devas …
sn56.11:12.6 | S: the gods of Yama … | T: the Yama devas …
sn56.11:12.7 | S: the joyful gods … | T: the Tusita devas …
sn56.11:12.8 | S: the gods who love to create … | T: the Nimmanarati devas …
sn56.11:12.9 | S: the gods who control what is created by others … | T: the Paranimmitavasavatti devas …
sn56.11:12.10 | S: the gods of the Divinity’s host raised the cry: | T: the devas of Brahma’s company raised a cry:
sn56.11:12.11 | S: “Near Varanasi, in the deer park at Isipatana, the Buddha has rolled forth the supreme Wheel of Dhamma. And that wheel cannot be rolled back by any ascetic or brahmin or god or Māra or divinity or by anyone in the world.” | T: “At Baraṇasi, in the Deer Park at Isipatana, this unsurpassed Wheel of the Dhamma has been set in motion by the Blessed One, which cannot be stopped by any ascetic or brahmin or deva or Mara or Brahma or by anyone in the world.”
sn56.11:13.1 | S: And so at that moment, that second, that hour, the cry soared up to the realm of divinity. | T: Thus at that moment, at that instant, at that second, the cry spread as far as the brahma world,
sn56.11:13.2 | S: And this ten-thousandfold galaxy shook and rocked and trembled. And an immeasurable, magnificent light appeared in the world, surpassing the glory of the gods. | T: and this ten thousandfold world system shook, quaked, and trembled, and an immeasurable glorious radiance appeared in the world surpassing the divine majesty of the devas.
sn56.11:14.1 | S: Then the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then the Blessed One uttered this inspired utterance:
sn56.11:14.2 | S: “Koṇḍañña has really understood! Koṇḍañña has really understood!” | T: “Koṇḍañña has indeed understood! Koṇḍañña has indeed understood!”
sn56.11:14.3 | S: And that’s how Venerable Koṇḍañña came to be known as “Koṇḍañña Who Understood”. | T: In this way the Venerable Koṇḍañña acquired the name “Añña Koṇḍañña—Koṇḍañña Who Has Understood.”
sn56.11:14.4 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

