sn56.13:1.3 | S: The noble truths of suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: The noble truth of suffering, the noble truth of the origin of suffering, the noble truth of the cessation of suffering, the noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering.
sn56.13:2.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of suffering? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of suffering?
sn56.13:2.2 | S: You should say: ‘The five grasping aggregates’. | T: It should be said: the five aggregates subject to clinging;
sn56.13:2.3 | S: That is: form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness. | T: that is, the form aggregate subject to clinging … the consciousness aggregate subject to clinging.
sn56.13:2.4 | S: This is called the noble truth of suffering. | T: This is called the noble truth of suffering.
sn56.13:3.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of the origin of suffering? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of the origin of suffering?
sn56.13:3.2 | S: It’s the craving that leads to future lives, mixed up with relishing and greed, taking pleasure there wherever it alights. That is, | T: It is this craving which leads to renewed existence, accompanied by delight and lust, seeking delight here and there; that is,
sn56.13:3.3 | S: craving for sensual pleasures, craving for existence, and craving for nonexistence. | T: craving for sensual pleasures, craving for existence, craving for extermination.
sn56.13:3.4 | S: This is called the noble truth of the origin of suffering. | T: This is called the noble truth of the origin of suffering.
sn56.13:4.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of the cessation of suffering? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of the cessation of suffering?
sn56.13:4.2 | S: It’s the fading away and cessation of that very same craving no residue left behind; giving it away, letting it go, releasing it, and not clinging to it. | T: It is the remainderless fading away and cessation of that same craving, the giving up and relinquishing of it, freedom from it, nonreliance on it.
sn56.13:4.3 | S: This is called the noble truth of the cessation of suffering. | T: This is called the noble truth of the cessation of suffering.
sn56.13:5.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering?
sn56.13:5.2 | S: It is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: It is this Noble Eightfold Path; that is,
sn56.13:5.3 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view … right concentration.
sn56.13:5.4 | S: This is called the noble truth of the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: This is called the noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering.
sn56.13:5.5 | S: These are the four noble truths. | T: “These, bhikkhus, are the Four Noble Truths.
sn56.13:6.1 | S: That’s why you should practice meditation to understand: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’.” | T: “Therefore, bhikkhus, an exertion should be made to understand: ‘This is suffering.’… An exertion should be made to understand: ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.’”
