## sn56.15
sn56.15:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, do you remember the four noble truths that I taught?” | T: “Bhikkhus, do you remember the Four Noble Truths taught by me?”
sn56.15:1.2 | S: When he said this, one of the mendicants said to the Buddha: | T: When this was said, a certain bhikkhu said to the Blessed One:
sn56.15:1.3 | S: “I do, sir.” | T: “Venerable sir, I remember the Four Noble Truths taught by the Blessed One.”
sn56.15:1.4 | S: “How so, mendicant?” | T: “But how, bhikkhu, do you remember the Four Noble Truths taught by me?”
sn56.15:1.5 | S: “Sir, I remember that suffering is the first noble truth you’ve taught; | T: “I remember suffering, venerable sir, as the first noble truth taught by the Blessed One.
sn56.15:1.6 | S: the origin of suffering is the second; | T: I remember the origin of suffering as the second noble truth taught by the Blessed One.
sn56.15:1.7 | S: the cessation of suffering is the third; | T: I remember the cessation of suffering as the third noble truth taught by the Blessed One.
sn56.15:1.8 | S: and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering is the fourth. | T: I remember the way leading to the cessation of suffering as the fourth noble truth taught by the Blessed One.
sn56.15:1.9 | S: That’s how I remember the four noble truths as you’ve taught them.” | T: It is in this way, venerable sir, that I remember the Four Noble Truths taught by the Blessed One.”
sn56.15:2.1 | S: “Good, good, mendicant! | T: “Good, good, bhikkhu!
sn56.15:2.2 | S: It’s good that you remember the four noble truths as I’ve taught them. | T: It is good that you remember the Four Noble Truths taught by me.
sn56.15:2.3 | S: Suffering is the first noble truth I’ve taught, and that’s how you should remember it. | T: Suffering, bhikkhu, is the first noble truth taught by me: remember it thus.
sn56.15:2.4 | S: The origin of suffering is the second; | T: The origin of suffering is the second noble truth taught by me: remember it thus.
sn56.15:2.5 | S: the cessation of suffering is the third; | T: The cessation of suffering is the third noble truth taught by me: remember it thus.
sn56.15:2.6 | S: and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering is the fourth. | T: The way leading to the cessation of suffering is the fourth noble truth taught by me: remember it thus.
sn56.15:2.7 | S: That’s how you should remember the four noble truths as I’ve taught them. | T: In this way, bhikkhu, remember the Four Noble Truths taught by me.
sn56.15:3.1 | S: That’s why you should practice meditation …” | T: “Therefore, bhikkhu, an exertion should be made to understand: ‘This is suffering.’… An exertion should be made to understand: ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.’”
sn56.15:3.2 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn56.17
sn56.17:1.1 | S: Seated to one side, that mendicant said to the Buddha: | T: Sitting to one side, that bhikkhu said to the Blessed One:
sn56.17:1.2 | S: “Sir, they speak of this thing called ‘ignorance’. | T: “Venerable sir, it is said, ‘ignorance, ignorance.’
sn56.17:1.3 | S: What is ignorance? | T: What is ignorance, venerable sir,
sn56.17:1.4 | S: And how is an ignoramus defined?” | T: and in what way is one immersed in ignorance?”
sn56.17:1.5 | S: “Not knowing about suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: “Bhikkhu, not knowing suffering, not knowing the origin of suffering, not knowing the cessation of suffering, not knowing the way leading to the cessation of suffering:
sn56.17:1.6 | S: This is called ignorance. | T: this is called ignorance, bhikkhu,
sn56.17:1.7 | S: And this is how an ignoramus is defined. | T: and it is in this way that one is immersed in ignorance.
sn56.17:2.1 | S: That’s why you should practice meditation …” | T: “Therefore, bhikkhu, an exertion should be made to understand: ‘This is suffering.’… An exertion should be made to understand: ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.’”
sn56.17:2.2 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn56.18
sn56.18:1.1 | S: Then a mendicant went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him: | T: Then a certain bhikkhu approached the Blessed One, paid homage to him, sat down to one side, and said to him:
sn56.18:1.2 | S: “Sir, they speak of this thing called ‘knowledge’. | T: “Venerable sir, it is said, ‘true knowledge, true knowledge.’
sn56.18:1.3 | S: What is knowledge? | T: What is true knowledge, venerable sir,
sn56.18:1.4 | S: And how is a knowing one defined?” | T: and in what way has one arrived at true knowledge?”
sn56.18:1.5 | S: “Knowing about suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: “Bhikkhu, knowledge of suffering, knowledge of the origin of suffering, knowledge of the cessation of suffering, knowledge of the way leading to the cessation of suffering:
sn56.18:1.6 | S: This is called knowledge. | T: this is called true knowledge, bhikkhu,
sn56.18:1.7 | S: And this is how a knowing one is defined. | T: and it is in this way that one has arrived at true knowledge.
sn56.18:2.1 | S: That’s why you should practice meditation …” | T: “Therefore, bhikkhu, an exertion should be made to understand: ‘This is suffering.’… An exertion should be made to understand: ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering.’”
