## sn56.21
sn56.21:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Vajjis at the village of Koṭi. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was dwelling among the Vajjians at Koṭigama.
sn56.21:1.2 | S: There the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: There the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus thus:
sn56.21:1.3 | S: “Mendicants, due to not understanding and not penetrating four noble truths, both you and I have wandered and transmigrated for such a very long time. | T: “Bhikkhus, it is because of not understanding and not penetrating the Four Noble Truths that you and I have roamed and wandered through this long course of saṁsara.
sn56.21:2.1 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn56.21:2.2 | S: The noble truths of suffering, | T: “It is, bhikkhus, because of not understanding and not penetrating the noble truth of suffering that you and I have roamed and wandered through this long course of saṁsara.
sn56.21:2.3 | S: the origin of suffering, | T: It is because of not understanding and not penetrating the noble truth of the origin of suffering …
sn56.21:2.4 | S: the cessation of suffering, | T: the noble truth of the cessation of suffering …
sn56.21:2.5 | S: and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: the noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering that you and I have roamed and wandered through this long course of saṁsara.
sn56.21:2.6 | S: These noble truths of suffering, origin, cessation, and the path have been understood and comprehended. Craving for continued existence has been cut off; the leash to existence is ended; now there’ll be no more future lives.” | T: “That noble truth of suffering, bhikkhus, has been understood and penetrated. That noble truth of the origin of suffering has been understood and penetrated. That noble truth of the cessation of suffering has been understood and penetrated. That noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering has been understood and penetrated. Craving for existence has been cut off; the conduit to existence has been destroyed; now there is no more renewed existence.”
sn56.21:3.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn56.21:3.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the Fortunate One, the Teacher, further said this:
sn56.21:4.1 | S: “Because of not truly seeing | T: “Because of not seeing as they are
sn56.21:4.2 | S: the four noble truths, | T: The Four Noble Truths,
sn56.21:4.3 | S: we have transmigrated for a long time | T: We have wandered through the long course
sn56.21:4.4 | S: from one rebirth to the next. | T: In the various kinds of births.
sn56.21:5.1 | S: But now that these truths have been seen, | T: “Now these truths have been seen;
sn56.21:5.2 | S: the leash to existence is eradicated. | T: The conduit to existence is severed;
sn56.21:5.3 | S: The root of suffering is cut off, | T: Cut off is the root of suffering:
sn56.21:5.4 | S: now there’ll be no more future lives.” | T: Now there is no more renewed existence.”
sn56.21:5.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn56.22
sn56.22:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are ascetics and brahmins who don’t truly understand about suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path. | T: “Bhikkhus, those ascetics or brahmins who do not understand as it really is: ‘This is suffering’; who do not understand as it really is: ‘This is the origin of suffering’; who do not understand as it really is: ‘This is the cessation of suffering’; who do not understand as it really is: ‘This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering’:
sn56.22:1.2 | S: I don’t deem them as true ascetics and brahmins. Those venerables don’t realize the goal of life as an ascetic or brahmin, and don’t live having realized it with their own insight in this very life. | T: these I do not consider to be ascetics among ascetics or brahmins among brahmins, and these venerable ones do not, by realizing it for themselves with direct knowledge, enter and dwell, in this very life, in the goal of asceticism or the goal of brahminhood.
sn56.22:2.1 | S: There are ascetics and brahmins who do truly understand about suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path. | T: “But, bhikkhus, those ascetics or brahmins who understand these things:
sn56.22:2.2 | S: I deem them as true ascetics and brahmins. Those venerables realize the goal of life as an ascetic or brahmin, and live having realized it with their own insight.” | T: these I consider to be ascetics among ascetics and brahmins among brahmins, and these venerable ones, by realizing it for themselves with direct knowledge, enter and dwell, in this very life, in the goal of asceticism and the goal of brahminhood.”
sn56.22:3.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn56.22:3.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the Fortunate One, the Teacher, further said this:
sn56.22:4.1 | S: “There are those who don’t understand suffering | T: “Those who do not understand suffering,
sn56.22:4.2 | S: and suffering’s cause, | T: Who do not know suffering’s origin,
sn56.22:4.3 | S: and where all suffering | T: Nor where suffering completely stops,
sn56.22:4.4 | S: ceases no residue left behind. | T: Where it ceases without remainder;
sn56.22:5.1 | S: And they do not know the path | T: Who do not know that path
sn56.22:5.2 | S: that leads to the stilling of suffering. | T: Which leads to suffering’s appeasement:
sn56.22:5.3 | S: They lack the heart’s release, | T: They are devoid of mind’s liberation
sn56.22:5.4 | S: as well as the release by wisdom. | T: And also of liberation by wisdom;
sn56.22:5.5 | S: Unable to make an end, | T: Incapable of making an end,
sn56.22:5.6 | S: they continue to be reborn and grow old. | T: They fare on to birth and aging.
sn56.22:6.1 | S: But there are those who understand suffering | T: “But those who understand suffering,
sn56.22:6.2 | S: and suffering’s cause, | T: Who know too suffering’s origin,
sn56.22:6.3 | S: and where all suffering | T: And where suffering completely stops,
sn56.22:6.4 | S: ceases no residue left behind. | T: Where it ceases without remainder;
sn56.22:7.1 | S: And they understand the path | T: Who understand that path
sn56.22:7.2 | S: that leads to the stilling of suffering. | T: Which leads to suffering’s appeasement:
sn56.22:7.3 | S: They’re endowed with the heart’s release, | T: They are endowed with mind’s liberation
sn56.22:7.4 | S: as well as the release by wisdom. | T: And also with liberation by wisdom;
sn56.22:7.5 | S: Able to make an end, | T: Being capable of making an end,
sn56.22:7.6 | S: they don’t continue to be reborn and grow old.” | T: They fare no more in birth and aging.”
sn56.22:7.7 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)


